  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 1227 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nThe early risers on Monday morning will wake up to higher \nhumidity and warmer morning lows. We will look to start the \nworkweek returning to those uncomfortable heat index days typical \nof September.\n\nStrong hurricane Simon in the eastern Pacific is forecast to \ncrash into the rugged terrain of southern Mexico Sunday or Sunday \nnight. An upper-level trough moving into the western CONUS is \nnudging slowly eastward and is eventually expected to push the \nupper ridge east, bringing a front to the area late next week. \nMeanwhile, tropical moisture pooling over the western Gulf will \nwrap around the western periphery of the subtropical ridge, which \ncontinues to provide relentless heat over our region. Heat index \nvalues will climb back into the 100 to 108-degree range for many \nareas, and this pattern will hold through at least Wednesday. \nThere is sufficient moisture to generate diurnally driven isolated\nto widely scattered showers and storms along and east of the I-35\ncorridor, but it doesn't appear to be getting any significant \nboost from the remnants of Simon. \n\nBy late Wednesday, the pattern begins to shift as the upper ridge\nmoves east. This finally introduces an opportunity to tap into a \npiece of Simon's remnant moisture. The current guidance only \naccounts for increasing rain chances over the Edwards Plateau \nstarting Wednesday night. The latest couple deterministic model \ncycles have the global models trending drier for the late week \npre- frontal environment, despite some remnant moisture and \ninstability from Simon remaining hung up in the high terrain to \nthe west. Two factors appear to coincide with this drier trend: \nfirst, a northward and weaker trend with the western CONUS trough;\nsecond, a westward- moving TUTT low coming in stronger and \nfarther north with each successive run. Frontal timing has also \nbeen highly inconsistent with this pattern change, but for now, it\nappears there will be at least some boost in rain chances \nThursday through Friday, with a cold front arriving sometime \nduring this period.",
